What the program does
Analyzes a large server request log file.
Converts raw request data into a performance summary report.
Mainly designed to process large amounts of data efficiently.
Input
Reads data from requests.log.
Each request contains:
Timestamp
HTTP method
Endpoint/API path
HTTP status code
Response latency
What it analyzes
Groups requests by:
Hour
Endpoint
Calculates:
Total number of requests
P50 latency — typical/median response time
P95 latency — response time for 95% of requests
P99 latency — response time for 99% of requests
Number of 5xx server errors
Error rate
How it handles large files
Doesn't load the entire log into memory at once.
Reads the file in chunks.
Splits the work into different sections.
Uses multiple CPU processes when appropriate.
Combines the results from different processes.
Performance optimization
Uses optimized data structures and algorithms.
Detects available CPU resources.
Can use CPU/cgroup limits to determine how much processing is safe.
Uses os.pread() for efficient file reading.
Uses optimized methods for calculating percentiles.
Can use posix_fadvise() when available to improve file-access behavior.
Error handling
Checks whether timestamps and log entries are valid.
Detects malformed lines.
Has multiple processing paths.
If the highly optimized method fails, it can fall back to simpler processing.
Output
Creates summary.txt.
The summary contains information such as:
Hour
Endpoint
Request count
P50
P95
P99
5xx errors
Error rate
Overall structure

requests.log → Read → Parse → Group → Analyze → Calculate statistics → Combine results → summary.txt

In one sentence
It is a high-performance Python server-log analyzer that turns huge amounts of raw request data into useful performance and error statistics.
